\DOC INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC

\TYPE {INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Simultaneous induction over a family of types defined together.

\DESCRIBE
A goal that is a conjunction of statements, one about each type of a
family of datatypes that were defined together, is exactly what the
induction theorem of that family concludes. {INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC} aims
that theorem at such a goal and splits the premises into one subgoal
each, with the arguments of the constructor fixed and the induction
hypotheses assumed.

This is the induction a proof about mutually recursive syntax needs, and
what {INDUCT_ON_TAC} cannot give: there the predicates of the other types
of the family are taken to be true, so the hypotheses about them are
lost. Rocq writes such a scheme out with {Scheme ... with ...}; HOL4
calls it {Induct_on} with the conjunction already there.

\FAILURE
Fails unless every conjunct of the goal is universally quantified over a
variable, and the type of the first of them is an inductive type whose
induction theorem matches the goal.

\EXAMPLE
With {exp} and {elist} defined together and {count}/{lcount} recursive
over both:
{
  # g `(forall e. 0 < count e) /\ (forall l. 0 <= lcount l)`;;
  # e INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC;;
  val it : goalstack = 4 subgoals (4 total)

    0 [`0 < count a0`]
    1 [`0 <= lcount a1`]

  `0 <= lcount (Cons a0 a1)`

  `0 <= lcount Nil`

    0 [`0 <= lcount a`]

  `0 < count (Call a)`

  `0 < count (Num a)`
}
\noindent The {Call} case carries the hypothesis about the {elist} in it,
which is the point of the strong scheme.

\SEEALSO
induction_for_type, induction_of, induction_shape, INDUCT_ON_TAC.

\ENDDOC
